        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Important opportunities for space travel in the decades ahead include a proposed mission to Mars. However, an important deterrent to such interplanetary travel is the risk of radiation-induced carcinogenesis. There are many uncertainties in predicting the risk of space travel because the mechanisms of carcinogenesis from space radiation, which is dominated by high-energy protons and high (H) charge (Z) and energy (E) (HZE) particles, are poorly understood. Investigators will test the hypothesis that microRNAs that regulate the expression of proteins that are known to regulate radiation-induced cancer, will function as non-protein encoding genetic factors that mediate radiation carcinogenesis from HZE particles. <p>
This study had the following specific aims:<ol>
<li>Investigate radiation carcinogenesis in mice lacking microRNA-182.
<li>Investigate radiation carcinogenesis in mice overexpressing microRNA-182.</ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The investigators bred, infected, and irradiated various strains of genetically engineered mice to assess the effect of miR-182 loss or overexpression on tumorigenesis. These mice were treated with multiple doses and fractionations of x-rays at Duke University and 56Fe and 28Si particles at Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL). Investigators tested the hypothesis that microRNAs that control the expression of proteins known to regulate radiation-induced cancer will function as genetic factors in radiation carcinogenesis. In order to study the role of microRNA-182 (miR-182) in radiation-induced lymphomagenesis and lung tumorigenesis following exposure to x-ray or space irradiation, they developed a mouse model that lacks miR-182 expression or expresses an elevated level of miR-182.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Investigators did not observe a significant impact of miR-182 expression level on the latency or the incidence of radiation-induced lymphomas. In the absence or presence of radiation, they found that loss of miR-182 reduced overall survival when compared to littermates that retained wild type expression of miR-182. From these data, the investigators conclude that miR-182 functions to regulate the survival of lung-tumor bearing mice. However, they have not seen evidence that there is a significant interaction between miR-182 expression and radiation exposure on survival. Consistent with their hypothesis that miR-182 promotes radiation-induced tumorigenesis, mice overexpressing miR-182 developed lymphomas at an accelerated rate post radiation exposure.
<p>
No datasets exist for this experiment. A final report was archived.
<p>
